\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.3 Illustrative Functional Enrichment Pattern\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 If the ten illustrative hub candidates in Table 2 were genuinely the output of a real network analysis, KEGG and Gene Ontology enrichment of the full predicted-target list would plausibly highlight pathways consistent with the biology already known for these protein families - the PI3K-Akt and MAPK signalling pathways (via AKT1, MAPK1), the HIF-1 and AMPK signalling pathways (via HIF1A, PRKAA1), TNF and NF-kB signalling (via TNF, RELA), and glutathione metabolism / oxidative stress response pathways (via NFE2L2). This is offered strictly as a plausibility check on the illustrative hub list, consistent with the general literature on these protein families - not as an enrichment result, since no enrichment analysis was actually run.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.4 Illustrative Docking Affinity Pattern\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Were the docking protocol in Section 3.5 genuinely carried out, it would return a binding-affinity estimate, in kcal/mol, for each compound-target pair docked. Published docking studies of structurally comparable flavonoids and phenolic compounds against comparable target classes commonly report binding affinities in the broad range of roughly -6 to -10 kcal/mol, with more negative values conventionally read as more favourable predicted binding. Table 3 illustrates the qualitative pattern such a study's output table would take - relative, not absolute, since the specific numbers shown are demonstration values, not docking results for these five compounds against these ten targets.\par
