\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.5 What This Illustrative Pattern Would Mean, If It Were Confirmed\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Read purely as a demonstration of interpretation, a confirmed version of the pattern sketched above would suggest a multi-target profile in which quercetin and mangiferin contribute the strongest predicted engagement with growth-factor and survival signalling (AKT1/MAPK1) and inflammatory hub nodes (TNF/IL6/STAT3), anacardic acid contributes complementary anti-inflammatory engagement consistent with its documented enzyme-inhibitory activity, and the bamboo-derived C-glycosylflavones orientin and vitexin contribute relatively more toward the oxidative-stress and energy-sensing axis (NFE2L2/PRKAA1) - a division of labour across the three botanical sources that would echo, at the level of network pharmacology, the same complementary-mechanism logic this journal has previously discussed for a different bamboo-leaf combination (Korie, 2026). Whether this pattern reflects anything real about these five compounds and these ten targets is precisely the question that running the framework in Section 3 - and not this article - would answer.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 5. Discussion\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.1 Why Publish a Framework Without Results\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Network pharmacology and molecular docking studies have become common enough in the natural-products literature that the methodological template itself - which databases to query, in what order, with which thresholds - is now reasonably well established, even though individual papers rarely spell every step out in enough detail for independent replication. Publishing the framework on its own, decoupled from any specific claimed result, serves a narrower but genuine purpose: it gives other researchers, particularly those working with limited computational resources or just beginning to apply these tools to African ethnobotanical material, a complete, named, reproducible protocol to follow, together with a worked example that shows what correctly interpreted output looks like before they have generated any of their own. This is the same logic that justified publishing a literature-synthesis-only paper elsewhere in this journal (Korie, 2026): doing the preparatory, hypothesis-shaping work openly and honestly, clearly labelled as preparatory, before the resource-intensive primary work begins.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.2 The Risk This Framing Is Designed to Avoid\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Network pharmacology and docking studies carry a specific, well-documented risk: because the computational pipeline produces numbers - node counts, centrality scores, binding energies - that look identical in format whether or not the underlying analysis was actually run with care, it is comparatively easy for such numbers to be presented with more confidence than the underlying work supports, or, in the worst case, fabricated outright. This article was deliberately structured specifically to avoid that risk: every quantitative figure in Section 4 is qualitatively hedged ("example: moderate," "illustrative," explicit rank labels marked "(illustrative)") rather than presented as a precise number with false authority, and the methodological notice repeated at the start of the document, in the Abstract, and again before Section 4 carries the same caveat in case a reader arrives at any one section without having read the others. Readers, reviewers, and anyone citing this work should treat that repetition as intentional rather than redundant.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.3 Limitations\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 The limitations of this article are, by design, total with respect to empirical findings: there are none, because none were generated. What can be assessed is the quality of the framework itself. The protocol specified in Section 3 follows widely accepted practice in the published network pharmacology literature, but it is not the only defensible version of such a pipeline - reasonable researchers differ on confidence thresholds, on which centrality measure or combination of measures best identifies genuine hubs, and on how many top-ranked targets merit docking follow-up. The illustrative target list in Section 2.3 and Table 2 is drawn from the general literature on structurally related compounds and is not, and cannot be, a substitute for an actual target-prediction query against this specific compound set; a genuine run could easily surface a substantially different hub list. Equally, the qualitative docking pattern in Table 3 should not be treated as even a weak prior on the real outcome of a genuine docking study - it illustrates how to read a results table, not what such a table would actually contain.\par
