\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.5 Relevance to Nigerian and African Computational Pharmacology Capacity\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Every tool named in Section 3 - PubChem, ChEMBL, SwissTargetPrediction, STRING, Cytoscape, the relevant R or Python enrichment packages, AutoDock Vina, and the Protein Data Bank - is free to access and runs on modest computing hardware, which makes this kind of framework genuinely usable by researchers and graduate students working in Nigerian and other African institutions without specialist wet-laboratory funding or high-performance computing infrastructure. Mangifera indica, Anacardium occidentale, and Bambusa species are all common, inexpensive, and widely cultivated across Nigeria, which lowers the practical barrier to eventually pairing any computational findings with locally sourced extracts for the in vitro and in vivo confirmation work described in Table 4. Making the methodology explicit and freely reusable, rather than treating it as a black box behind a results table, is itself a small contribution toward building that capacity.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 6. Conclusion\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 This article specifies a complete, reproducible network pharmacology and molecular docking framework for examining multi-target synergy between Anacardiaceae leaf compounds (mangiferin and quercetin from Mangifera indica; anacardic acid from Anacardium occidentale) and Poaceae leaf compounds (orientin and vitexin from Bambusa spp.), and illustrates, through a fully labelled worked example, what each stage of that framework's output would look like and how it should be interpreted. It deliberately stops short of claiming any empirical result: no target-prediction query, network construction, enrichment analysis, or docking run was executed for this specific compound set, and every quantitative figure in Section 4 is marked as illustrative for exactly that reason.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 What this article offers instead is a named, citable, step-by-step protocol - built entirely from real, publicly available tools and databases - together with the interpretive scaffolding needed to read its eventual output correctly, and a staged roadmap (Table 4) showing exactly what primary computational and laboratory work remains before any claim about this combination's actual multi-target mechanism could be responsibly made. Whether mangiferin, quercetin, anacardic acid, orientin, and vitexin genuinely converge on the hub targets sketched illustratively in Section 4 is an open, answerable, and inexpensive question for the next researcher - in Nigeria or elsewhere - willing to run the framework for real.\par
